                        }
                      }
                    ],
                    "text": "Killarney"
                  },
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": ", Co. Kerry."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "2026 Wildfire impacts"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This year’s wildfire impacts will not be fully known until 2027, but media reports have noted impacts including:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Over 100ha of Coillte forests impacted by fire in 2026,"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Wildfires in Ireland are burning protected nature areas,"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "25ha of Killarney National Park damaged and visitors barred,"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "300ha of Wicklow mountains national park damaged,"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Retailers asked to completely halt the sale of disposable barbecues,"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "N71 road shut down with related traffic and tourism impacts,"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Smoke blanketing Dublin suburbs, and,"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "HSE warning of air quality health risks."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "bold"
              }
            ],
            "text": "What can we do?"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Fire management is complex, and there is no “silver bullet” solution. Broadly, fire management comes down to:"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "bulletList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Suppression – reduction of ignition sources (e.g. bans on campfires, barbecues, uncontrolled land burning) and firefighting when fires ignite,"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Fuel removal – grazing and controlled burns to remove vegetation,"
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Land use adaptation – protection & restoration of fire-resistant ecosystems (native woodland or peatlands)."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "While Ireland already implements suppression, there is room for increased implementation of fuel removal and land use adaptation strategies."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Small-scale grazing programmes used to suppress fire risk have been implemented (e.g. Old Irish goats on Dalkey and Howth), and the "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "link",
                "attrs": {
                  "href": "https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-agriculture-food-and-the-marine/",
                  "target": "_blank",
                  "rel": "noopener noreferrer nofollow",
                  "class": null,
                  "title": null
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "Department of Agriculture, Food and the Marine"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " has allowed some grazing changes to "
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "marks": [
              {
                "type": "link",
                "attrs": {
                  "href": "https://www.gov.ie/en/department-of-agriculture-food-and-the-marine/campaigns/agri-climate-rural-environment-scheme-acres/",
                  "target": "_blank",
                  "rel": "noopener noreferrer nofollow",
                  "class": null,
                  "title": null
                }
              }
            ],
            "text": "ACRES"
          },
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": " actions in 2026, though primarily to address pressure on winter fodder. Grazing to manage landscapes has been used in the Burren and Connemara, and declining upland grazing due to changing farming policy has been identified as a factor increasing wildfire risk in Ireland. Upland grazing programmes that balance fuel removal goals while minimising soil erosion and resulting flood risk have the potential to deliver “win-win-win” for farmers, fire management and climate resilience."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Ireland’s peatlands are often the site of wildfires, and ~82% are already degraded to some degree. Emerging global evidence has shown that rewetted and restored peatlands are more resistant to wildfires, (Figure 6), delivering another “win-win-win” by mitigating fires, managing water and reducing carbon emissions."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Ireland has already shown it can successfully restore peatlands, and is required to rewet its drained organic soils under the Nature Restoration Law, allowing a policy opportunity to manage wildfire risk by strategic rewetting."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
